//
// Module map for XCTestCore.framework.
//
// This file is installed as XCTestCore.framework/Modules/module.modulemap, so
// the umbrella header below is resolved relative to the framework's Headers
// directory. It is what makes "@import XCTestCore;" work for the xctest agent
// and for anything else that links the private side of a test run.
//
// The umbrella is XCTestCore.h rather than one of the headers it imports.
// A module map declares exactly one umbrella, and naming any of the others would
// leave the rest reachable only by naming their files -- which works, and is not
// something a client should have to know.
//
// This framework holds every implementation file: the test machinery
// (XCTestCase, XCTestSuite, XCTestRun, the expectations, the assertions, the
// metrics) as well as the run configuration, the selection, and the loader.
// XCTest.framework is the public half: it installs the headers and the umbrella,
// contains no objects of its own, and re-exports this framework. Apple's
// dependency runs the same way round -- XCTest re-exports XCTestCore, and
// XCTestCore does not link XCTest -- and there is nothing to add here, because
// the direction that matters is a test bundle links XCTest, XCTest links
// XCTestCore, and nothing ever links back.
//
// One deliberate difference from Apple: this framework installs a Headers
// directory and this module map, and Apple's does not. Apple's XCTestCore
// Versions/A contains only the binary, Resources, and _CodeSignature, so
// "@import XCTestCore;" cannot work against it at all. The private umbrella is
// kept because it is the clean way to reach these declarations from the agent
// that drives a run once that lands; nothing outside this repository can tell
// the difference, because the public API is reached through XCTest in both.

framework module XCTestCore {
  umbrella header "XCTestCore.h"
  export *
  module * { export * }
}
